            <ArticleTitle>Prevalence of respiratory symptoms in children and air quality by village in rural Indonesia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1174-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This study compared prevalence of respiratory symptoms in three Indonesian villages and related this to air quality.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We interviewed caregivers of 382 children, using the International Study of Asthma and Allergies in Childhood (ISAAC) questionnaire, and monitored air quality during the survey period.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Respiratory symptom prevalence was highest in Kerinci (40.5%), followed by SP7 (33.3%) and Pelalawan (19.8%). Compared with Pelalawan, adjusted odds ratios were 3.17 (95% confidence interval, 1.43-7.07) for Kerinci, and 2.03 (1.04-3.96) for SP7. Ambient air quality levels were highest in Kerinci for PM10 and hydrocarbon (means: 102.9 microg/m3, 10.5 microg/m3), followed by SP7 (73.7 microg/m3, 6.3 microg/m3) and Pelalawan (26.1 microg/m3, 4.7 microg/m3).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The higher prevalence of respiratory symptoms in Kerinci and SP7 could be the result of higher PM10 and hydrocarbon levels in these locations.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
